WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.743
This lesson gives area a physical meaning.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:11.486
On a speed-time graph, the area under the graph equals the distance travelled.

3
00:00:11.486 --> 00:00:17.229
That is because each small strip has a speed height and a time width, so each strip contributes a small distance.

4
00:00:17.229 --> 00:00:22.972
When all the strips are combined, the total shaded area becomes the total distance for the interval.

5
00:00:22.972 --> 00:00:28.715
Once that meaning is secure, rectangles, triangles, and trapezia stop feeling like random geometry tasks.

6
00:00:28.715 --> 00:00:34.458
Keep this takeaway in view: On a speed-time graph, the full area under the line gives total distance.

7
00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:40.201
Graph type means speed-time.

8
00:00:40.201 --> 00:00:45.944
Area gets its meaning from the axes, not from the word graph alone.

9
00:00:45.944 --> 00:00:51.687
Shaded region means total distance.

10
00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:57.430
The full area under the graph is the total distance travelled.

11
00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:03.060
The area diagram is powerful because it turns the motion story into visible blocks and wedges.

12
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:08.690
Every part of the shaded region contributes to the total distance.

13
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:14.320
A rectangle shows constant speed for a time interval.

14
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.950
A triangle or trapezium shows a changing speed across the interval.

15
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:25.580
The full shaded region matters, not just the top edge of the graph.

16
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:31.210
That is the central idea the worked example is about to use.

17
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.840
Keep this takeaway in view: Distance comes from the full area, even when the graph shape changes along the way.

18
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.470
Rectangle region: Constant speed times time gives a distance block.

19
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:48.100
Sloped region: Changing speed can still be handled by a triangle or trapezium area.

20
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.376
A strong analogy is a strip-built distance meter.

21
00:01:53.376 --> 00:01:58.653
Picture the graph area sliced into many thin vertical strips.

22
00:01:58.653 --> 00:02:03.929
Each strip has a tiny time width and a speed height.

23
00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:09.205
Speed times time gives a small distance for that strip.

24
00:02:09.205 --> 00:02:14.482
Add all the strips and you get the total distance.

25
00:02:14.482 --> 00:02:19.758
This analogy matters because it shows why even unusual shapes can still represent total distance: every strip still contributes its share.

26
00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:25.035
Keep this takeaway in view: Area means accumulated distance because each thin strip contributes speed x time.

27
00:02:25.035 --> 00:02:30.311
Thin strip means small time x speed.

28
00:02:30.311 --> 00:02:35.587
Each strip adds a little piece of distance.

29
00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:40.864
Many strips means total distance.

30
00:02:40.864 --> 00:02:46.140
Adding the strips gives the whole journey distance.

31
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.702
In practice, learners usually split the graph into familiar shapes.

32
00:02:51.702 --> 00:02:57.263
A rectangle gives speed times time.

33
00:02:57.263 --> 00:03:02.825
A triangle gives one-half base times height.

34
00:03:02.825 --> 00:03:08.387
A trapezium gives one-half open bracket a plus b close bracket times h, or average speed times time.

35
00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:13.948
The important thing is not to worship the geometry.

36
00:03:13.948 --> 00:03:19.510
The point is that each area is representing a distance contribution from that section of the motion story.

37
00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:25.072
Keep this takeaway in view: Choose shapes to represent distance contributions cleanly, then add them.

38
00:03:25.072 --> 00:03:30.633
Rectangle: Best for constant-speed sections.

39
00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:36.195
Triangle: Useful when the line rises or falls from zero in a straight way.

40
00:03:36.195 --> 00:03:41.757
rectangle: area = speed x time.

41
00:03:41.757 --> 00:03:47.318
triangle: area = 1/2 x base x height.

42
00:03:47.318 --> 00:03:52.880
Choose the area shape that matches the graph section, then add all sections for the total distance.

43
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.328
One of the deepest ideas in this lesson is that different speed-time shapes can still represent the same total distance if the total area matches.

44
00:03:58.328 --> 00:04:03.776
One graph may have a tall narrow peak.

45
00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:09.224
Another may have a lower wider region.

46
00:04:09.224 --> 00:04:14.672
If the full areas are equal, the total distance is equal even though the speed histories are different.

47
00:04:14.672 --> 00:04:20.120
This helps learners stop judging total distance from the highest point alone.

48
00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.568
Keep this takeaway in view: Equal total area means equal total distance even when the graphs look different.

49
00:04:25.568 --> 00:04:31.016
Tall narrow shape means one speed story.

50
00:04:31.016 --> 00:04:36.464
A sharper pace pattern can still produce a certain total area.

51
00:04:36.464 --> 00:04:41.912
Lower wider shape means another speed story.

52
00:04:41.912 --> 00:04:47.360
A gentler pace pattern can match the same total area.

53
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.085
A major trap in this lesson is to look at the graph's final height and think that number must be the total distance.

54
00:04:53.085 --> 00:04:58.810
But the final height is just the final speed on a speed-time graph.

55
00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:04.535
Total distance comes from the area under the graph over the full interval.

56
00:05:04.535 --> 00:05:10.260
Another trap is to calculate one section and forget to add the rest.

57
00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.985
The lesson wants full-area thinking, not last-point thinking.

58
00:05:15.985 --> 00:05:21.710
Keep this takeaway in view: Distance comes from the full area, not from the last point on the line.

59
00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:27.435
Final graph height gives final speed, not total distance.

60
00:05:27.435 --> 00:05:33.160
Area meaning belongs specifically to speed-time graphs.

61
00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.605
Check yourself.

62
00:05:36.605 --> 00:05:40.051
Why does area have meaning here?

63
00:05:40.051 --> 00:05:43.496
Because the axes are speed and time.

64
00:05:43.496 --> 00:05:46.942
What gives the total distance?

65
00:05:46.942 --> 00:05:50.387
The full shaded region under the graph.

66
00:05:50.387 --> 00:05:53.832
Can two different shapes give the same total distance?

67
00:05:53.832 --> 00:05:57.278
Yes, if their total areas are equal.

68
00:05:57.278 --> 00:06:00.723
Those three answers are the complete logic of the lesson.

69
00:06:00.723 --> 00:06:04.168
Keep this takeaway in view: Area, not height, is the distance story on this graph type.

70
00:06:04.168 --> 00:06:07.614
Why area works means speed x time.

71
00:06:07.614 --> 00:06:11.059
The axes give the area a distance meaning.

72
00:06:11.059 --> 00:06:14.505
What to use means whole shaded region.

73
00:06:14.505 --> 00:06:17.950
Total distance comes from all sections together.

74
00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:23.762
You are ready for the worked example now.

75
00:06:23.762 --> 00:06:29.573
The graph shows six metres per second for four seconds, then a straight rise from six to ten metres per second over the next two seconds.

76
00:06:29.573 --> 00:06:35.385
Start with the first section: rectangle area equals six times four, which gives twenty-four metres.

77
00:06:35.385 --> 00:06:41.196
Then use the second section as a trapezium.

78
00:06:41.196 --> 00:06:47.008
Average speed is open bracket six plus ten close bracket over two, which is eight.

79
00:06:47.008 --> 00:06:52.819
Multiply by two seconds to get sixteen metres.

80
00:06:52.819 --> 00:06:58.631
Add the two areas: twenty-four plus sixteen gives forty metres total distance.

81
00:06:58.631 --> 00:07:04.442
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: build total distance from the whole area under the graph.

82
00:07:04.442 --> 00:07:10.254
Step 1: Split the graph into manageable shapes that match each section.

83
00:07:10.254 --> 00:07:16.065
Step 2: Find the distance contribution from each area separately.

84
00:07:16.065 --> 00:07:21.877
rectangle: 6 x 4 = 24 m.

85
00:07:21.877 --> 00:07:27.688
trapezium: ((6 + 10) / 2) x 2 = 16 m.

86
00:07:27.688 --> 00:07:33.500
The total distance comes from adding the area contributions of all graph sections.
